          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "**Is this urgent?** The urgency check found no emergency pattern in your readings, so the advice is routine. Book a non-urgent appointment with your clinician to review your readings. You do not need to act tonight. The check is not a full clearance, so do not treat this as reassurance that everything is fine. A research review of the same question notes that 150/95 falls in the \"stage 2\" range under the US classification in *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, which covers office readings of 140 or higher systolic or 90 or higher diastolic. That guidance does not give a home-reading threshold or a timeframe for contacting a clinician, so your clinician will decide how quickly to follow up.\n\n**Call emergency services right away** if you get chest pain, trouble breathing, a sudden severe headache, vision changes, confusion, or weakness or numbness on one side of the body. The source documents do not list these warning signs, so this is a general safety precaution. Also tell a clinician promptly about any new symptoms, or if your readings climb well above your usual range.\n\n**What the target should be.** The sources disagree on this point, and the difference seems to come from what each one retrieved, not from a clear guideline conflict. One source says US guidance sets a target below 130/80 mm Hg for most treated adults, with looser targets for older adults with frailty, limited life expectancy, or several other conditions. The other source found no general adult target in its material. It found only a target below 130/80 mm Hg for people with coronary artery disease, which is a condition-specific target. Your clinician can tell you which target applies to you.\n\n**When to start treatment.** Authorities also differ here. Some guidance advises lifestyle changes alone for lower-risk people with stage 1 readings, according to *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*. Another source says some guidance begins treating at 140/90. Your reading falls in the stage 2 range under the US classification, so this difference matters less for you. Before treatment is stepped up, the guidance says readings should be confirmed outside the clinic and adherence and blood-pressure-raising medicines should be reviewed. Home readings count toward diagnosis when averaged over two or more readings on two or more occasions.\n\n**Things you can do now.** Reducing salt is one of the best-studied changes. Most salt comes from packaged and restaurant food rather than the shaker, according to *Diet for high blood pressure*. Regular aerobic activity also helps, with a common target of 150 minutes a week of moderate activity such as brisk walking, per *Physical activity for high blood pressure*. None of the sources graded how strong the evidence is behind these targets or thresholds.\n\n**Next step:** Bring your home log to your appointment, including the dates, times, readings, and how each was taken. Your clinician can confirm the readings and decide on your target.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
